{"id": "section-body-03921", "title": "Rabies", "chapterId": "chapter-four", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "NOTIFIABLE DISEASES", "Viral Infections", "Rabies"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-03921", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Rabies", "runs": [{"text": "Rabies"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1802"}}, {"id": "body-03922", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Rabies is a zoonotic disease (a disease that is transmitted to humans from animals eg. dogs) that is caused by a virus. Rabies infects domestic and wild animals and is spread to people through close contact with infected saliva (via bites or scratches) The rabies virus infects the central nervous system, causing disease in the brain and, eventually, death. Early symptoms in people include: fever, headache, and general weakness or discomfort. As the disease progresses, symptoms include; insomnia, anxiety, confusion, slight or partial paralysis, excitable behavior, hallucinations, increase in saliva, difficulty swallowing, and fear of water (hydrophobia). In unvaccinated humans, rabies is almost always fatal if post-exposure prophylaxis is not administered before the onset of severe symptoms.", "runs": [{"text": "Rabies"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "zoonotic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "disease"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "disease"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "transmitted"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "humans"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "from"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "animals"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "eg."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dogs)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "caused"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "virus."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Rabies infects domestic and wild animals and is spread to people through close contact with infected saliva (via bites or scratches) The rabies virus infects the central nervous system, causing disease in the brain and, eventually, death. Early symptoms in people include: fever, headache, and general weakness or discomfort. As the disease progresses, symptoms include; insomnia, anxiety, confusion, slight or partial paralysis, excitable behavior, hallucinations, increase in saliva, difficulty swallowing, and fear of water (hydrophobia). In unvaccinated humans, rabies is almost always fatal if post-exposure prophylaxis is not administered before the onset of severe symptoms."}]}, {"id": "body-03924", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Case Definition", "runs": [{"text": "Case"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Definition"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-03925", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Suspected: A person with one or more of the following: headache, neck pain, nausea, fever, fear of water, anxiety, agitation, abnormal tingling sensations or pain at the wound site, when contact with a rabid animal is suspected.", "runs": [{"text": "Suspected:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "A"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "person with one or more of the following: headache, neck pain, nausea, fever, fear of water, anxiety, agitation, abnormal tingling sensations or pain at the wound site, when contact with a rabid animal is suspected."}]}, {"id": "body-03926", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Confirmed: A suspected case that is laboratory confirmed", "runs": [{"text": "Confirmed:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "A"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "suspected"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "case"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "laboratory"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "confirmed"}]}, {"id": "body-03929", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Laboratory Investigation", "runs": [{"text": "Laboratory"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Investigation"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-03930", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Human rabies is confirmed by intra-vitam and post mortem diagnostic techniques that detect whole viruses, viral antigens, or nucleic acids in infected tissues (brain, skin or saliva). E.g., Direct fluorescent antibody (DFA) test, which targets rabies virus antigens in brain tissue, Identification of viral nucleic acid by reverse transcriptase PCR on fixed tissue collected post mortem or in a clinical specimen (brain tissue or skin, cornea or saliva) or Detectable rabies-neutralizing antibody titre in the CSF of an unvaccinated person.", "runs": [{"text": "Human"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "rabies"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "confirmed"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intra-vitam"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "post"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mortem"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "diagnostic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "techniques"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "detect"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "whole"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "viruses,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "viral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "antigens,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or nucleic acids in infected tissues (brain,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "skin or saliva). E.g., Direct"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "fluorescent"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "antibody (DFA) test,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "which targets rabies virus antigens"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "brain"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tissue,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Identification"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "viral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "nucleic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "acid"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "reverse"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "transcriptase"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "PCR"}, {"text": " "